        "id": "about_chapter_7-1",
        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 7: PowerShell and the Web \u2013 HTTP, REST, and JSON\nP\u00e1ginas: 188-215\n\u2022 Working with HTTP  (189)\n  \u2022 Why don\u2019t we see Forms information in PowerShell 7?  (193)\n  \u2022 Authentication  (195)\n\u2022 Getting to grips with APIs  (197)\n  \u2022 RPC APIs  (198)\n  \u2022 SOAP APIs  (199)\n  \u2022 REST APIs  (199)\n  \u2022 WebSocket APIs  (199)\n\u2022 Working with REST  (199)\n  \u2022 Invoke-RestMethod  (200)\n\u2022 Working with JSON  (203)\n  \u2022 ConvertFrom-Json  (205)\n  \u2022 ConvertTo-Json  (207)\n  \u2022 Test-Json  (210)\n\u2022 Let\u2019s have some fun \u2013 who is on the International Space Station?  (211)\n\u2022 Summary  (212)\n\u2022 Exercises  (213)\n\u2022 Further reading  (214)\n7\nPowerShell and the\nWeb \u2013 HTTP, REST, and JSON\nUp until now, we\u2019ve worked in a very small space \u2013 our client machine. It\u2019s nice, but the world doesn\u2019t\nwork like that. Pretty much every device we use is connected to other machines and also, usually, the\ninternet. Devices need to be able to interact with services provided over the web to download data,\ninteract with cloud applications, and play games. In this, chapter we\u2019re going to look at ways we can\nuse PowerShell to work with objects in the world outside our box.\nWe\u2019ll start out with a brief chat about web services and then look at the basic PowerShell tool for\ninteracting with them; Invoke-WebRequest . We\u2019ll go on to look at Application Programming\nInterfaces ( APIs ). We\u2019ll talk about the dominant type of API in use today \u2013 Representational\nState Transfer ( REST ). Once we\u2019ve got a good grip on that and the tool we can use to work with it,\nInvoke-RestMethod , we\u2019ll look at some of the ways of exchanging information with web services\nusing JavaScript Object Notation ( JSON ), and the tools we use to convert JSON data into PowerShell",
